
Q1. Explain the four pillars of OOPS —
 Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction. Give a real-world example for each.

 Ans -> Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming approach based on classes and objects.
  Its four main pillars are Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction.

  (i) Encapsulation -> Encapsulation is process of wrapping data into single unit and
                       restricting direct access using private keyword.

  (ii) Inheritance -> Inheritance allows one class to acquire the properties and methods of another class.
                      It promotes code reusability.

  (iii) Polymorphism -> Polymorphism means one name, many forms.
                        The same method name or interface can behave differently depending on the context.

  (vi) Abstraction -> Abstraction means hiding implementation details and showing only essential functionality.

Q2. Difference between Abstract Class and Interface in Java. When would you use each?

Ans ->  An abstract class is a class declared using the abstract keyword.
        It can contain both abstract methods and concrete methods.

        An interface defines a contract that implementing classes must follow.
        It can contain abstract methods, default methods, static methods, and constants.

Q3. Method Overloading vs Method Overriding.

Ans -> Method overloading means defining multiple methods with the same name but
       different parameter lists in the same class. It supports compile-time polymorphism.

       Method overriding means a subclass provides its own implementation of an inherited
       instance method with a compatible signature. It supports runtime polymorphism.

Q4. Difference between == and .equals() in Java.

Ans -> The == operator compares primitive values directly.
For objects, it checks whether both references point to the same object.
The .equals() method checks logical equality according to the class's implementation.

Q5. What are the different types of collections in Java? Explain List, Set, and Map.

Ans -> The Java Collections Framework provides classes and interfaces for storing and manipulating groups of objects.

List: An ordered collection that allows duplicate elements.
Examples: ArrayList, LinkedList.

Set: A collection that does not allow duplicate elements.
Examples: HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet.

Map: Stores data as key-value pairs. Keys must be unique, while values may be duplicated.
Examples: HashMap, LinkedHashMap, TreeMap.